Z||000000||rc5star||06-21-2001||12:10 PM||||I bought these wheels off of EBAY but they do not fit. <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=592576946" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=592576946</A> <P>The bolt pattern is ok but the center ring of the wheel is to small to fit on the fiero. Can I take these wheels to a shop and have them bored to a larger size to fit? <BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||TRiAD||06-21-2001||12:15 PM||||<B>The ad says they were already bored out to fit, and would fit the Fiero. <BR>I would make the seller refund the money, and look into legal action if he does not.</B><BR>...But I've been burned too many times, so I'm probably more cynical in that respect than most.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||4
Z||000002||rc5star||06-21-2001||12:51 PM||||They are the same wheels but from a different person. But I know what you mean about being burned!||||reg||1
Z||000003||EdsB52||06-21-2001||12:58 PM||||If you like the wheels, which I do, I would take them to have them machined to fit and then send a letter of demand to the seller for the reimbursement of the cost of machining. Actually, I might want to first notify the seller and give him the option of buying them back and paying postage/shipping both ways, and then if he doesn't respond or responds negatively, I would take the former action. Since it's interstate, it becomes a Federal crime to willfully misrepresent an item for sale and use public (or private) means to transport accross state lines. Not to mention the fact that the purchase was made on an interstate/global phone line or cable. The problem might be motivating a prosecutor to drive this case, but it could be done.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Alex4mula||06-21-2001||01:55 PM||||They may charge you $25 to $50 per wheel to machine  them. That would add $100 to $200 to the cost. Maybe wrong but I think that the machine cost plus the original cost may allow you to buy a new set of your like (16")at DiscountTire or similar place.<P>------------------<BR>Alex4mula<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/p69fbfe3e99ec5a0b81c10afcc578ff95/fe6d5c42.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||qwikgta||06-21-2001||10:32 PM||||I bought a 87 coupe with a set of Universal fit rims.  THey had a plastic piece that went on the middle that made them fit the fiero hub.  Now the rims were bigger so the piece was able to fit into the rim center and fit onto the fiero hub.  Not that this helps but there may be a piece that will allow the same thing for your situation. I would check with some rim/tire shops to see if they can help.  <P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 65K, Header no Cat, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, swaybar, open element air cleaner, hood vents.<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html<BR>http://vafiero.com||||reg||1
Z||000006||TRiAD||06-22-2001||09:28 AM||||That's called a "hub-centric ring". <P>It works to create a flush surface between the wheel and hub, it works on wheels made for it, but wouldn't work on a wheel with a hold too small for the hub. ||||reg||1
